A1. What a small boy goes in to when he has to take a bath. ~ TANTRUM
A6. Numerical creator of American nobility. ~ MILLION
A12. Many a high ball has been caught in one. ~ MIT
A13. If this is lost it shouldn't be advertised in the papers (abbr.). ~ REP
A15. By way of. ~ VIA
A16. A dumb yes. ~ NOD
A18. Indefinite article. ~ AN
A19. This helps the light-fingered gentry in the art of lifting watches. ~ FOB
A20. A side door Pullman tourist. ~ BUM
A22. It takes a little one to get an ocean liner started. ~ TUG
A24. An editor's pronoun. ~ WE
A25. A pronoun. ~ YOU
A26. Something that causes a lot of trouble. ~ RUMOR
A28. What Scotchmen hate to do (Scot.). ~ GIE
A30. Something opportunity isn't supposed to use. ~ DOORBELL
A32. A nickel nurser. ~ TIGHTWAD
A34. A vase. ~ URN
A35. The residence of one of God's frozen people. ~ IGLOO
A37. Before. (Poet.) ~ ERE
A38. Position on a football team (abbr.). ~ FB
A39. What runners get before they go. ~ SET
A41. Something girls put jewelry in. ~ EAR
A42. They play this on a tennis court. ~ SET
A44. This comes before goodness. (Exl.). ~ MY
A45. These get full when baseball season starts. ~ BASES
A47. How bouncers put people out. ~ THROW
A49. Kitty's boy friend. ~ TOM
A50. To work at steadily. ~ PLY
A51. An elusive vegetable. ~ PEA
A52. What the members of a Ladies Aid do to the absent members. ~ PAN
A54. A characteristic of reformers. ~ NOSEY
A57. The color of an amber pipestem. ~ AMBER
A60. Spanish definite article. ~ EL
A62. Something Eve did that caused a lot of trouble for her boy friend. ~ ATE
A63. Girl's name. ~ MAE
A65. What ball players do when they make errors. ~ ERR
A66. The original settler (of bills). ~ PA
A67. Man's nickname. ~ NED
A69. A bird that is a great worm catcher. ~ EARLY
A71. A horse laugh. ~ HAW
A72. A very disliked kind of howler. ~ CALAMITY
A74. Composer of sheet music. ~ SLEEPERS
A76. A rustic. ~ YAP
A77. An old-fashioned girl's name. ~ ABBIE
A79. What babies do when bachelors hold them. (Be careful now!) ~ CRY
A80. The printer's measure. (Sorry, but we couldn't help it.) ~ EM
A82. The kind of a skin Captain John Smith loved to touch. ~ RED
A84. Before. ~ ERE
A85. Pronoun made famous by Rider Haggard. ~ SHE
A86. A note of the scale. ~ MI
A87. Our benevolent uncle. ~ SAM
A89. This kind of light has a high scandal power. ~ DIM
A91. Part of a golf course difficult to get away from. ~ TEE
A92. This is cracked. ~ NUT
A93. A noisy party. (Nothing to do with native sons.) ~ REVELRY
A94. What husbands get when they come home at 3 A.M. ~ ROASTED

D1. Money (slang). ~ TIN
D2. Preposition of place. ~ AT
D3. The trouble with these is that there are too many of them. ~ TROUBLES
D4. A Confederate in the Civil War (Colloq.) ~ REB
D5. Where aeroplanes go. ~ UP
D7. This comes between three and five. ~ IV
D8. Lamp-posts can get this way and still be perfectly steady. ~ LIT
D9. What "he who laughs last," hears before he starts laughing. ~ LAUGHTER
D10. Where repentant soaks get when the water wagon starts. ~ ON
D11. The best time to turn over a new leaf. ~ NOW
D12. The Scot who "laid on." ~ MACDUFF
D14. A well-known demon. ~ RUM
D17. First name of Dick who never missed his aim. ~ DEADEYE
D19. Conjunction meaning because. ~ FOR
D20. Protuberance on the hip. ~ BULGE
D21. Money make the mare go but it takes gas to make this go. ~ MOTOR
D23. Go! (Gas house lingo.) ~ GIT
D25. Over there. (Poet.) ~ YON
D26. Roman lightning insurance. (Init.) (Try and get it!) ~ RLI
D27. A Spanish river. ~ RIO
D29. A progenitor of Mary's little lamb. ~ EWE
D31. A poet's eye. ~ ORB
D33. What every girl likes to have around her. ~ ARM
D36. A soft place to sit. ~ LAP
D39. Where flappers wear shredded wheat gowns. ~ SAMOA
D40. Domicile of the original 100 per cent. Americans. ~ TEPEE
D42. A synonym for a word meaning shame. ~ SHAME
D43. A souse. ~ TOPER
D45. This is good in France. ~ BON
D46. A characteristic of foxes and stock salesmen. ~ SLY
D47. A famous Boston party. ~ TEA
D48. This causes a lot of fighting. ~ WAR
D53. Hoboes' boudoirs. ~ BENCHES
D55. What a Follies girl would cause in an old men's home. ~ STAMPEDE
D56. An antique piece of mahogany furniture. ~ BAR
D58. Nowadays it's the wives who wear these. ~ BREECHES
D59. A lawyer's delight. ~ LAWSUIT
D61. A crossword meadow. ~ LEA
D63. This is a synonym for possibly and we don't mean perhaps. ~ MAYBE
D64. A girl's name that starts with an E. ~ ELSIE
D66. A difficult thing to make a hole in. ~ PAR
D68. American Chinese for lack of water. ~ DLY
D69. A Greek letter. ~ ETA
D70. Yankee liquid extinguisher. (Init.) This is very easy to get! ~ YLE
D71. A salutation used in Dubuque. ~ HEY
D73. Aaron (abbr.). ~ AAR
D75. A prefix of quality when applied to Volstead. ~ PRE
D78. What the Colonel and Mr. O'Grady are under a skin full. (Sing. abbr.) ~ BRO
D81. Deface. ~ MAR
D83. To calm. (Scot.) ~ DIL
D85. The place where breakers go broke. ~ SEA
D86. What politicians debate with. ~ MUD
D88. A popular pronoun. ~ ME
D90. An abbreviated symbol of masculinity. ~ MR
D91. Preposition. ~ TO
D92. Point of the compass. ~ NE


